*% For content longer than even the "-long" (150mm) presets, use macOS's
*% "Paper Size > Manage Custom Sizes..." and set the HEIGHT to match your
*% loaded tape width (this PPD declares pages wide: Width = label length,
*% Height = tape width -- the filter picks the tape width from the actual
*% page height in the print job, not this preset's name).
*MaxMediaWidth: "5000"
*MinMediaWidth: "20"
*MaxMediaHeight: "68"
*MinMediaHeight: "10"
*HWMargins: 0 0 0 0
*% Standard CUPS custom-page-size boilerplate (see cups/spec-ppd.html):
*% the print system pushes Width Height WidthOffset HeightOffset
*% Orientation onto the stack before running this code. The "pop pop
*% pop" is NOT optional -- it discards WidthOffset/HeightOffset/
*% Orientation before "[5 -2 roll]" reorders the remaining Width/Height
*% into a valid PageSize array. Missing it (an earlier version of this
*% PPD did) was confirmed on real hardware to badly corrupt the page
*% geometry for any Custom size -- content came out scaled/positioned
*% as if rotated -- while every named PageSize preset (mm12 etc.) was
*% unaffected, since those declare PageSize directly without going
*% through this code path at all.
*CustomPageSize True: "pop pop pop <</PageSize[5 -2 roll]/ImagingBBox null>>setpagedevice"
*ParamCustomPageSize Width: "1 points 20 5000"
*ParamCustomPageSize Height: "2 points 10 68"
*ParamCustomPageSize WidthOffset: "0 points 0 0"
*ParamCustomPageSize HeightOffset: "0 points 0 0"
*ParamCustomPageSize Orientation: "0 int 0 0"

*OpenUI *AutoCut/Cut Each Label: Boolean
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *AutoCut
*DefaultAutoCut: True
*AutoCut True/Yes: ""
*AutoCut False/No: ""
*CloseUI: *AutoCut

*% Trims blank tape a job's actual content doesn't use, down to just what
*% the content needs (read directly by the filter; not a real PostScript
*% page-size change, so it works together with whichever Tape Width/
*% Custom size you picked -- that just needs to be big enough for your
*% content, not an exact match). Turn off if you deliberately want the
*% full page size printed, blank space included.
*OpenUI *AutoLength/Automatic Length: Boolean
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *AutoLength
*DefaultAutoLength: True
*AutoLength True/Yes: ""
*AutoLength False/No: ""
*CloseUI: *AutoLength

*% The correct mirror correction (confirmed on real hardware) is now
*% baked directly into the filter's mandatory transpose (see
*% rastertoptp700) rather than left to this PPD default: lpadmin was
*% found not to reliably refresh an existing queue's cached PPD copy
*% (/etc/cups/ppd/<queue>.ppd kept a stale *DefaultImageMirror after
*% `lpadmin -p ... -P ...` on the same queue name), so a PPD-only default
*% wasn't dependable. These options now apply an ADDITIONAL rotate/mirror
*% on top of the built-in correction -- both default off (no-op) -- e.g.
*% to deliberately get the old vertical-reading orientation
*% (Rotate90/Rotate270), or to flip the built-in mirror back off again if
*% you ever need to (Rotate180+Mirror=True cancels it). Read directly by
*% the filter, not applied via setpagedevice/PostScript.
*OpenUI *ImageRotate/Rotate: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *ImageRotate
*DefaultImageRotate: None
*ImageRotate None/0 degrees: ""
*ImageRotate Rotate90/90 degrees: ""
*ImageRotate Rotate180/180 degrees: ""
*ImageRotate Rotate270/270 degrees: ""
*CloseUI: *ImageRotate

*OpenUI *ImageMirror/Mirror Image: Boolean
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *ImageMirror
*DefaultImageMirror: False
*ImageMirror True/Yes: ""
*ImageMirror False/No: ""
*CloseUI: *ImageMirror

*OpenUI *Resolution/Resolution: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Resolution
*DefaultResolution: 180dpi
*Resolution 180dpi/180 DPI: "<</HWResolution[180 180]>>setpagedevice"
*CloseUI: *Resolution

*OpenUI *ColorModel/Color Mode: PickOne
*OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *ColorModel
*DefaultColorModel: Gray
*% cupsColorSpace 3 = CUPS_CSPACE_K ("Black", an ink/toner model: higher
*% value = more ink = darker). NOT 0 (CUPS_CSPACE_W, "Luminance": higher
*% value = more light = whiter, the opposite of what a 1-bit-per-pixel
*% "1 = print black" printer driver wants). Confirmed on real hardware:
*% with cupsColorSpace 0, text printed as a photo negative -- a solid
*% black field with the actual glyph shapes showing through as unprinted
*% (white) gaps, because CUPS encoded text pixels as 0 (black/no light)
*% and background as 1 (white/full light), which this driver's raster
*% encoder reads straight through as 0 = don't print, 1 = print.
*ColorModel Gray/Black and White: "<</cupsColorSpace 3/cupsBitsPerColor 1/cupsColorOrder 0/cupsCompression 0>>setpagedevice"
*CloseUI: *ColorModel

*cupsBitsPerColor: 1
*cupsColorOrder: Chunky
*cupsColorSpace: K
*cupsCompression: 0
